{"body":"**[suggestion]** `kernel_calls()` is locked to `NR_KERN_CALLS_PHASE4` and contiguity, but each server band’s `members` vector is not checked against its `NR_*` count. Adding e.g. `PM_GETSID` and bumping `NR_PM_MSGS` on the Rust side will update the C `NR_PM_MSGS` define and the band-ordering `_Static_assert`s, while silently omitting the new `PM_GETSID` macro until someone remembers to extend `bands()`. That is exactly the drift class D8 was meant to prevent for the C view of the ABI.

**Suggestion:** In `every_band_base_member_and_count_appears` (or a sibling test), assert `band.members.len() == count` whenever `band.count` is `Some`, and for the VM band assert `members` still ends at `VM_FORK` / matches the Rust last-member convention used by the SEF ordering assert.","id":3650591232,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":254,"original_line":254,"path":"tools/gen-c-headers/src/callnr_h.rs","user":"KevinBarnard"}
{"body":"**[suggestion]** `processes()` is a hand-written fixed array of 16 `(name, ProcNr)` pairs with no completeness lock against `com::NR_TASKS + com::NR_BOOT_PROCS` (or an equivalent enumeration of every well-known boot slot). A new boot server added to `com.rs` and packed into MXBI would get a Rust endpoint and boot image entry, but `minix/com.h` would not grow `_PROC_NR` / `_EP` macros until this table is edited. The existing tests only verify that whatever is listed renders correctly.

**Suggestion:** Add a unit test (or `const _: ()`-style host assert) that `processes().len() == com::NR_TASKS + com::NR_BOOT_PROCS`, and that the listed proc numbers are exactly the task slots plus `0..NR_BOOT_PROCS`. Optionally generate names from a single table shared with the renderer so a new row is one edit.","id":3650591233,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":18,"original_line":18,"path":"tools/gen-c-headers/src/com_h.rs","user":"KevinBarnard"}
{"body":"**[nit]** Payload length, `sizeof`, `_Alignof`, and field offsets are all taken from the live `MessageLayout::measure()`, but the struct still hardcodes `_Alignas(8)` in the emitted C. Today that matches `l.align` and the snapshot test (`assert_eq!(l.align, 8)`), so CI would still fail if alignment drifted (via `_Alignof(message) == …`), but the declaration itself would briefly disagree with the measured layout until someone notices the hardcode.

**Suggestion:** Emit `_Alignas({})` from `l.align` the same way `payload[{}]` uses `l.payload_len`, so every layout number in the generated text is measurement-driven.","id":3650591236,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":130,"original_line":130,"path":"tools/gen-c-headers/src/ipc_h.rs","user":"KevinBarnard"}

